From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMCX <noreply@openmcx.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ims_ipsec_pcscf: TS 33.203 Rel-19 algorithms, algorithm list,
 checked SAs and protected port guards

- add_sa(): the integrity and encryption algorithms of TS 33.203 V19.1.0
  annex H with the key expansion of annex I and TS 33.210 V19.3.0 clauses
  5.3.3/5.3.4: hmac-sha-1-96, hmac-sha2-256-128 (KDF of TS 33.220 annex B),
  aes-gcm and aes-gmac (AEAD rfc4106/rfc4543 with the KDF salt) and their
  unique-salt "-us" variants; hmac-md5-96 and des-ede3-cbc, removed from
  annex H, are refused. The kernel's answer is checked (NLM_F_ACK), as for
  add_policy(): a refused SA no longer goes unnoticed.
- create_ipsec_tunnel(): a tunnel whose SAs or policies fail is deleted and
  the creation fails, so the 401 never announces SAs that do not exist
  (TS 33.203 clause 7.2).
- parse_sec_agree() replaced: every Security-Client header field and every
  mechanism in it is parsed on its own (the former parser merged the
  parameters of all mechanisms); the P-CSCF selects the first combination of
  its own ordered list (new parameter ipsec_algorithms) that the UE offers,
  and its Security-Server lists the whole list (TS 33.203 clause 7.2, SM6).
- add_port_guards(), at start-up: BLOCK policies for the protected ports
  below the SAs' policies, so that no unprotected message is received on or
  sent from them (TS 33.203 clause 7.1).
- update_contact_ipsec_params(): a re-registration's new set keeps the
  port_ps of the set in use (TS 33.203 clause 7.4.0), and the P-CSCF's SPIs
  differ from the UE's (clause 7.1).
- create_ipsec_tunnel(): only the two pairs of SAs of clause 6.1.0; the four
  cross-port SAs, which the kernel refused as duplicates, are gone.
- destroy_ipsec_tunnel(): a set is destroyed once, its SPIs given back once,
  so that the old set of a re-registration can be deleted early (clause
  7.4.2a) without its contact's expiry releasing the SPIs again.
- ipsec_destroy_when_idle(): TS 33.203 clause 7.4.2a, "The old SAs are then
  deleted as soon as all pending SIP transactions have been completed, or
  have timed out", and the new SAs after a failed authentication: tm
  callbacks count the transactions started over each set (requests from the
  UE on port_uc, requests to the UE from port_pc); a released set is deleted
  when its count is 0.
- ipsec_set_released(): TS 33.203 clause 6.1.5, a REGISTER over a set being
  deleted is not over an SA of the latest successful authentication.
- ipsec_sa_pair_free(): TS 33.203 clause 7.1 rule 3, the pair (UE address,
  protected client port) must not be in the SA table already.
- spi_gen: the SA_table of TS 33.203 clause 7.1 rule 1: each SPI pair in
  use holds the record of its set (UE address, ports, SPIs, the IMPI of its
  REGISTER, temporary until ipsec_set_completed() at SM12); the pool uses a
  Kamailio shared-memory lock instead of a POSIX mutex not shared between the
  processes, and gives a pair back once only (a second release put it twice
  in the free list; clause 7.1, distinct SPIs). A copy of a set whose set is
  gone deletes and releases nothing.
- ipsec_create(): TS 33.203 clauses 7.3.1.4 and 7.4.2a, "The registration
  SAs shall be deleted if they exist": when the S-CSCF's challenge arrives,
  the temporary sets of the same UE registration (same IMPI, same UE address)
  are deleted before the new set is created, the one the challenged REGISTER
  came over once its transaction has ended; a temporary set ends after
  ipsec_temp_set_lifetime (TS 24.229 clause 5.2.2.2, 401 item 4:
  reg-await-auth, 4 minutes), also a re-registration's, which no contact
  holds (clauses 7.3.1.1 and 7.4.2a). A set that cannot be recorded or
  admitted is deleted again and the creation fails.
- ipsec_impi_reserve(): TS 33.203 clause 7.1 rule 3, "for any one IMPI, no
  more than six SAs per direction are stored at any one time": counting and
  admission are one step under the pool's lock and bound to the REGISTER's
  transaction; the challenge's set consumes the admission, the transaction's
  end gives back an unused one. Counted: the IMPI's sets that stay over all
  its contacts and the admissions of other transactions; at most three sets
  (two SAs per direction each). ipsec_sa_pair_free() checks the pair against
  the same records.
- ipsec_set_completed(), ipsec_forward(): TS 33.203 clause 7.4.2a after
  SM12: if SM1 came over an old valid set, that set stays in use for
  outbound messages and the other old sets are deleted; the UE's first
  request or response over the new set's inbound SA takes the new set into
  use and the old one goes once its pending transactions are over; when the
  old set ends with its lifetime, the new one is used. If SM1 was
  unprotected, the old sets are removed. Responses go over the set their
  request came on.
- ipsec_reply_over_sa(): a stateful reply to a request received over a set
  leaves over UDP from the set's port_pc to the UE's port_us (clause 7.1;
  clauses 7.4.1a/7.4.2a, failure messages over the old SAs).

The modified files carry a notice of the change (GPL-2.0 section 2(a)).
---
diff --git a/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c b/src/modules/ims_ipsec_pcscf/cmd.c
